hem saying it's not just  good enough to be able to know that you  know when I have this certain formula up  here I follow the formula I plug it in  and I get an answer right I know you  know why that formula comes about and  forms other formulas etc and right so  we're really aiming for in this  classical pedagogy right is this fusion  of both of these into one sort of  central combination of procedural and  conceptual the other thing that I think  it's a fusion of is this idea of things  that are done by the teacher they're  delivered by the teacher to students  right as well as this idea of student  driven explorations so this might be  something that I as a teacher sort of  really guiding my students through I'm  asking them a bunch of questions here  I'm the one that's kind of making making  the material come alive but I also don't  want to just hand in the answers right  well my favorite quotes about  mathematics is mathematics is the art of  explanation right if we do all the  explaining to the students right  we actually rob them of the mathematics  themselves itself right and so what we  really want is our students to be able  to explore and to drive their  exploration into the topics right if you  think about teaching multiplication  right I don't I do want to be able to  tell the students you know  multiplication is this active repeated  addition but I also want them to be able  to experience multiplication right I  want them to be able to say to answer  their question you know how do we figure  out how many feet we have as a class  right and get them counting by twos and  get them being the ones saying I don't  need to ask every single person how many  feet they have every one has two feet  and so I can do two times the number of  people in the class right and so we have  this student driven sort of exploration  right and I think the fusion here that  lies at the center is this idea of these  kind of conversations right and so the  teachers are asking these pointed these  meaningful questions to students and the  students are engaging in those things by  engaging in those questions with their  own ideas right and so me as a teacher  is this dynamic in engagement with the  students around this meaningful topic  right  so I think you are right math is math is  math right it's always been math it's  the same kind of content but I think  what separates this idea of classical  mathematics is  not just jamming content into kids heads  over and over and over again until they  get it and we're not just turning them  loose to say go for go to explore all  the math that you want to write it's  this dynamic fusion of the what's and  the whys right within this kind of  conversation type structure right and  that I think is really what's gonna make  classical what make mathematics  classical
 

